WEBVTT

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:07.610
Follow along as I knit one side
of my baby cardigan using a knit radar.

00:00:07.643 --> 00:00:10.850
Shape your knitting
by following along the lines.

00:00:10.883 --> 00:00:17.290
My gauge is 28 stitches to 4 inches, and
I'm using the appropriate stitch scale.

00:00:17.323 --> 00:00:20.880
On the mylar, the drawing is aligned
to the left at the zero

00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:23.920
mark of the stitch scale.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.930
To get started, the instruction
is cast on 30 stitches.

00:00:28.963 --> 00:00:31.800
The numbers and the tick marks
on the scale represent

00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:35.080
the needles on the needle bed.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.050
We'll cast on needles,
right 1 to right 30,

00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:42.930
and then I'll continue knitting without
any shaping until I get

00:00:42.963 --> 00:00:45.770
to the arm hole shaping.

00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:49.810
At this point, I'm reading that I need
to bind off, decrease,

00:00:49.843 --> 00:00:52.930
or short row three stitches.

00:00:52.963 --> 00:00:58.690
Notice that the line is between
the three and four marks on the scale.

00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:03.160
Now, this is not an exact science,
so I'll use my judgment and choose to bind

00:01:03.193 --> 00:01:06.890
off three stitches and make notes as I go.

00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:10.970
Because I'm going to be duplicating this
shaping while knitting the other cardigan

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:17.120
front, I'll note my shaping
right on the mylar as I knit.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.960
After knitting two rows,
the curved arm hole line is indicating

00:01:20.993 --> 00:01:25.170
that I need to bind off,
short row or decrease two more stitches.

00:01:25.203 --> 00:01:29.610
The tick mark is indicating
right needle number five.

00:01:29.643 --> 00:01:33.010
Knitting two more rows,
I'll decrease one stitch,

00:01:33.043 --> 00:01:35.440
right needle number six.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.650
Knitting two more rows, decrease one
stitch, right needle number seven.

00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:46.560
Now you can use whatever shaping methods
you'd like decreasing

00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:49.210
bind off or short rows.

00:01:49.243 --> 00:01:52.250
This is just giving
you the numbers to use.

00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:56.040
We've decreased a total of eight stitches.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.240
Following the straight section
of that arm hole opening,

00:02:02.273 --> 00:02:09.240
I'll continue to knit to row 57
until I see this neckline shaping.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.210
Now remember to document
your shaping as you work.

00:02:13.243 --> 00:02:17.920
This time I'm going to decrease
one stitch on the right side.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.730
I'll knit two rows, decrease two stitches,
knit two rows,

00:02:24.763 --> 00:02:30.610
decrease or short row five stitches,
knit two rows, decrease two,

00:02:30.643 --> 00:02:34.050
knit two rows, and decrease two.

00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:37.050
I'm choosing to do
my shaping every other row.

00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:42.090
You can choose to do your shaping
in other intervals if you'd like.

00:02:42.123 --> 00:02:47.200
Just follow the lines so you get
the shaping that you drew on your mylar.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.680
As my neck opening shaping is complete,
there will be stitches on needle

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:55.370
right nine through right 19.

00:02:55.403 --> 00:03:00.240
Knit over these 10 stitches
for the shoulders and then bind

00:03:00.273 --> 00:03:03.570
off or scrap off on row 68.

00:03:03.603 --> 00:03:07.240
This completes one side of my cardigan.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.290
Now to knit the other side of the
cardigan, we'll reverse the shaping.

00:03:13.323 --> 00:03:17.850
We'll cast on left 1 through left 30.

00:03:17.883 --> 00:03:21.680
Instead of shaping the arm hole
on the left, we're going to shape the arm

00:03:21.713 --> 00:03:26.440
hole on the right, following
the lines and the needle numbers.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.970
Then we'll shape the neck line on the
opposite edge of the arm hole shaping.

00:03:36.003 --> 00:03:43.050
If this is confusing for you,
draw a left and right side on your mylar,

00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:48.320
but remember to duplicate the shaping
instructions so both sides match.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.680
Keeping track of the stitch counts for our
cardigan fronts was

00:03:53.713 --> 00:03:55.330
pretty straightforward.

00:03:55.363 --> 00:03:59.570
If we were knitting for an adult,
even at one-half scale, the sleeve

00:03:59.603 --> 00:04:02.330
probably really won't fit on the mylar.

00:04:02.363 --> 00:04:07.650
So we'll just draw half of the sleeve
and half of the sweater back.

00:04:07.683 --> 00:04:10.130
As we're knitting,
we'll still follow the lines,

00:04:10.163 --> 00:04:14.170
but we'll mirror the shaping
on both sides of the needle bed.

00:04:14.203 --> 00:04:21.410
For example, on this sleeve, I'll cast on
40 stitches, needles right 20 and left 20.

00:04:21.443 --> 00:04:24.320
The shaping is mirrored
on each side of zero.

